KG42.register("rules-38eb81fda37a8cf570c765ee",{"id":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-c-R006","paper":"41-data_center_local_markets","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R006","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Prosumer participation","process_stage":"participation","market_meaning":"The IDCs may participate as energy prosumers, and the CSP may sell their surplus energy to other end energy prosumers through the local energy markets hosting the IDCs. Scope: Proposed CSP framework managing geographically distributed IDCs through the local energy markets hosting those IDCs. Condition: The studied IDCs integrate renewable energy and energy storage; energy sold is surplus energy. Local-market supply participation is absent; surplus energy generated by the IDCs is fed back into the grid without subsidy. Scope: Benchmark Case 2 only. Condition: The local energy trading mechanism is absent. The CSP cannot sell energy to the local energy markets in this benchmark case. Scope: Benchmark Case 3 only. Condition: Both BESSs and renewable energy sources are absent.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function participation.prosumer under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["participation.prosumer"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Benchmark Case 2 only. | Benchmark Case 3 only. | Proposed CSP framework managing geographically distributed IDCs through the local energy markets hosting those IDCs.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E01","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g02-E19"]},"x":[],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The IDCs may participate as energy prosumers, and the CSP may sell their surplus energy to other end energy prosumers through the local energy markets hosting the IDCs. Scope: Proposed CSP framework managing geographically distributed IDCs through the local energy markets hosting those IDCs. Condition: The studied IDCs integrate renewable energy and energy storage; energy sold is surplus energy.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E01","v141-b-g01-E04"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local-market supply participation is absent; surplus energy generated by the IDCs is fed back into the grid without subsidy. Scope: Benchmark Case 2 only. Condition: The local energy trading mechanism is absent.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The CSP cannot sell energy to the local energy markets in this benchmark case. Scope: Benchmark Case 3 only. Condition: Both BESSs and renewable energy sources are absent.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E01","location":{"location_id":"src-307a684414-p01-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"Abstract","anchor_text":"Yang Dong c na NSW 2052, Australia ork Internet Data Centers (IDCs) are large energy consumers in mo","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Active distribution network Internet Data Centers (IDCs) are large energy consumers in modern distribution networks. The integration of renewable energy and energy storage systems transforms IDCs into proactive energy prosumers, and enables them to participate in the energy trading in regional local energy markets. This paper proposes an energy management framework for a Cloud Service Provider (CSP) that manages multiple geographically distributed IDCs. The framework provides decision-making support for the CSP to optimally schedule the IDCs’ cyber-energy resources (computing requests, on-site battery energy storage systems, and on-site renewable energy sources). In this way, the proposed framework facilitates the CSP to proactively participate in the local energy markets situated in the regions that host the IDCs, with the objective to minimize the CSP’s total cost over a finite operation horizon."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-307a684414-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.1 System overview","anchor_text":"a ir In this study, we consider a CSP that manages K networked IDCs. The gy IDCs are geographically ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this study, we consider a CSP that manages K networked IDCs. The IDCs are geographically distributed in K different areas, where there is a local energy market in each area. The K networked IDCs can receive end users’ computing requests delivered by W third-party communication infrastructure providers. Each IDC is considered to be equipped with the following on-site IT and energy resources: (i) a number of servers that process computing requests; (ii) RESs, including photovoltaic solar panels and/or wind turbines; and (iii) a BESS that is used to accommodate the on-site renewable energy and provide energy backup support to the IDC. Through properly scheduling computing requests in the IDC network and controlling power outputs of the on-site energy resources, the CSP can alter the temporal-spatial power consumption and production distribution of the IDC network. This, in turn, allows the CSP to purchase deficit energy from and sell surplus energy to other end energy prosumers (e.g. buildings and electric vehicles) via multiple local energy markets that host the IDCs. Such vision is depicted in Fig. 1."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-307a684414-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.2 Energy management results","anchor_text":"each IDC s connected power line is set at 500 kW. All programs are implemented by Matlab and execute","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"For validation purpose, we compare the proposed energy management model with 3 benchmark cases:\n• Case 1 – the proposed model;\n• Case 2 – without local energy trading mechanism. That is, the CSP can only purchase energy from the grid, while the surplus energy generated by the IDCs will be fed back into the grid without subsidy;\n• Case 3 – without BESSs and renewable energy sources. In this case, the CSP can still purchase energy from both the grid and the local energy markets, but it cannot sell energy to the markets due to the absence of these facilities.\n• Case 4 – without temporal scheduling of computing requests. That is, each request is allocated to an IDC and is immediately processed in its arrival time slot."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":"No x object is declared in the frozen rule. The only graph object is the unary relation participation.prosumer; the source evidence states qualitative IDC/CSP prosumer roles and benchmark-case absences rather than a design selection or operational decision variable within this rule.","empty_theta_reason":"No theta object is declared in the frozen rule, and no fixed numerical parameter is used by its three prose constraints. The statements refer to the presence or absence of renewable energy, energy storage, and local energy trading mechanisms in words, without source-supported parameter symbols or values scoped to this rule."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-participation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"participation.prosumer","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E01","quote":"The integration of renewable energy and energy storage systems transforms IDCs into proactive energy prosumers, and enables them to participate in the energy trading in regional local energy markets."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","quote":"This, in turn, allows the CSP to purchase deficit energy from and sell surplus energy to other end energy prosumers (e.g. buildings and electric vehicles) via multiple local energy markets that host the IDCs."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","quote":"Case 2 – without local energy trading mechanism. That is, the CSP can only purchase energy from the grid, while the surplus energy generated by the IDCs will be fed back into the grid without subsidy;"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","quote":"Case 3 – without BESSs and renewable energy sources. In this case, the CSP can still purchase energy from both the grid and the local energy markets, but it cannot sell energy to the markets due to the absence of these facilities."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"76c3587dacf6913c38631336fbac1655b68814ab5bf91c56ae8d50090b4cfacb","applicability":"non_mathematical","reason":"The frozen rule consists of qualitative participant-role and benchmark-absence statements about IDCs/CSP acting as local-market prosumers, not a source-stated equation or optimization constraint. Its only graph object is the unary relation participation.prosumer, with no declared x/theta objects and no edges in V/E. The evidence describes permissible prosumer roles, surplus-energy sales, and Case 2/Case 3 absences in prose; no supported mathematical expression is present within this rule to formalize. The attached B evidence remains unreviewed, and this output does not assert independent source verification.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-C001","node_ids":["participation.prosumer"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C001 is the positive-scope statement that IDCs/CSP may act as local-market energy prosumers and sell surplus energy through the local markets hosting the IDCs. The frozen graph represents this role only through the unary participation.prosumer node; no edge exists to bind."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-C002","node_ids":["participation.prosumer"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C002 is the Benchmark Case 2 negative branch of the same prosumer-participation relation: local-market supply participation is absent and surplus energy is fed back to the grid without subsidy. It remains scoped to participation.prosumer, with no graph edge available."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-C003","node_ids":["participation.prosumer"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C003 is the Benchmark Case 3 negative branch of the prosumer-participation relation: the CSP cannot sell to local markets because BESSs and renewable sources are absent. It is a scope condition on participation.prosumer, and the frozen graph supplies no edge."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
